## Proof

**Claim.** Let $(X, O_X)$ be a scheme and $I \subset O_X$ an ideal sheaf with $I^n = 0$ (the zero sheaf). Then for every open set $U \subseteq X$, the ideal $I(U) \subset O_X(U)$ is nilpotent; in fact $I(U)^n = 0$.

**Key observation.** The sheaf $I^n$ is *by definition* the sheafification of the presheaf
$$P : U \longmapsto I(U)^n,$$
where $I(U)^n$ denotes the $n$-th power of the ideal $I(U)$ inside the ring $O_X(U)$. The crucial point is that the canonical map $P(U) \to P^{+}(U) = (I^n)(U)$ is **injective** for every open $U$, so the sheafification does not "lose" the information that $I(U)^n$ is nonzero.

**Step 1: The presheaf $P$ is separated.**

The presheaf $P$ is a sub-presheaf of $O_X$: for each $U$, the inclusion $I(U)^n \hookrightarrow O_X(U)$ is injective (it is the inclusion of an ideal into its ambient ring), and these inclusions are compatible with restriction. Since $O_X$ is a sheaf, it is separated: any two sections of $O_X(U)$ that agree on an open cover of $U$ must be equal. Because $P(U) \to O_X(U)$ is injective for all $U$, the presheaf $P$ inherits the separatedness property: if $s, t \in P(U)$ agree on an open cover, then they agree as elements of $O_X(U)$, hence $s = t$ in $P(U)$.

**Step 2: Sheafification of a separated presheaf is injective on sections.**

For any separated presheaf $P$, the canonical map $\varphi_U : P(U) \to P^{+}(U)$ to its sheafification is injective for every open $U$. This is a standard fact: $P^{+}(U)$ consists of compatible families of germs $(s_x)_{x \in U}$ that are locally represented by sections of $P$, and $\varphi_U$ sends $s \in P(U)$ to its family of germs $(s_x)_{x \in U}$. The map $\varphi_U$ is injective precisely when $P$ is separated, because $\varphi_U(s) = \varphi_U(t)$ iff $s_x = t_x$ for all $x \in U$ iff $s$ and $t$ agree locally iff $s = t$ (by separatedness).

**Step 3: Conclusion.**

Applying Steps 1 and 2 to $P(U) = I(U)^n$, we obtain an injection for every open $U$:
$$I(U)^n \;\xhookrightarrow{\;\varphi_U\;}\; (I^n)(U).$$

Since $I^n = 0$ as a sheaf, $(I^n)(U) = 0$ for every open $U$. By injectivity of $\varphi_U$:
$$I(U)^n = 0 \quad \text{for all open } U \subseteq X.$$

Therefore $I(U)$ is a nilpotent ideal in $O_X(U)$ for every open set $U$, with nilpotence index at most $n$. $\blacksquare$

**Remark.** The essential subtlety is that sheafification can in general *enlarge* the sections (adding sections that are only locally representable), but it cannot *identify* distinct sections when the presheaf is already separated. Since $I(U)^n$ sits inside the sheaf $O_X$, it is automatically separated, so no information is lost. The nilpotence $I^n = 0$ at the sheaf level therefore descends to nilpotence $I(U)^n = 0$ at the section level for every open $U$.

